3. The optimizer must be the SAS NLPQN dual BFGS (DBFGS) method, implemented from scratch in optimizer.py exactly as specified below. Do not substitute scipy.optimize.minimize, statsmodels, sklearn or any other fitting routine for the estimation.

   DBFGS spec:
   - Keep the Hessian approximation B (NOT the inverse Hessian) as a lower-triangular Cholesky factor L, with B = L @ L.T. Never form an explicit inverse.
   - Initial L = sqrt(c) * I where c = max(max(abs(g0)), 1.0), unless the SAS code sets an initial Hessian, in which case use its Cholesky factor.
   - Each iteration:
     a. z = solve_triangular(L, g, lower=True)
     b. Stop if max(abs(g)) <= ABSGCONV, or if (z @ z) / max(abs(f), 1e-6) <= GCONV. Also stop at MAXITER / MAXFUNC.
     c. Direction d = -solve_triangular(L.T, z, lower=False)
     d. Step size a from scipy.optimize.line_search (strong Wolfe). If it returns None, fall back to Armijo backtracking: start a=1, halve until f(x + a*d) <= f + 1e-4 * a * (g @ d).
     e. s = a*d, x_new = x + s, y = g_new - g
     f. If (y @ s) > 1e-10 * norm(s) * norm(y), update L:
        v = L.T @ s
        w = v / norm(v)
        J = L + outer(y / sqrt(y @ s) - L @ w, w)
        R = scipy.linalg.qr(J.T, mode="r")[0]
        L = R.T, then multiply each column of L by the sign of its diagonal element so the diagonal is positive.
        Otherwise skip the update and keep L.
